        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES<br><br>As space exploration missions journey further from Low-Earth orbit, crewmembers will be confronted with<br>longer communication delays with ground support. This lag time can be up to 20 minutes one way for Mars<br>missions. These delays degrade the space-ground communication process and make it more difficult for Mission<br>Control Center (MCC) to establish a common understanding with space crewmembers during their interactions.<br>Deep space exploration missions will necessitate increased crewmember autonomy however, MCC will still need<br>to collaborate with the crew to ensure their safety and address unforeseen problems that may arise during<br>missions that may jeopardize the success of the mission. Transmission speed is fixed by the laws of physics and,<br>therefore, it is necessary to explore solutions that focus on the communication process.<br>Previously, the investigators identified three critical features of asynchronous communication: Timing (when to<br>expect a response), Thread (tracking and maintaining conversational threads), and Transmission Efficiency<br>(‘chunking’ relevant information in a single message). This study builds upon the medium-specific<br>communication protocols and a training module that the investigators created to help space crews and MCC<br>personnel communicate and collaborate during time delay. Braiding is a novel, text-based software that was<br>developed to arrange communication sessions into threads, or ‘braids’ along a carousel. Remote team members<br>communicate with each other by viewing opposite sides of the carousel and interacting with the braid directly in<br>front of them. As each team member responds to the content on their braid, the carousel will rotate one step and<br>bring new content waiting for a response. Braiding is expected to help space crewmembers and MCC to organize<br>and track their time delayed communications, and thus to prevent topics from getting ‘tangled.’<br>In order to provide a comprehensive proof-of-concept, the investigators will explore two aims. During Aim 1, the<br>investigators will attempt to demonstrate the feasibility and benefits of the Braiding support system. In Aim 2, the<br>goal is to identify refinements and features required for use in space operations.<br><br>APPROACH<br><br>The investigators plan to recruit 8 to 12 volunteers to participate in a human-in-the loop (HITL) evaluation of<br>Braiding. Four to six volunteers will be assigned the role of crewmember and the remaining volunteers will act<br>as MCC personnel (flight surgeon or capcom, dependent on test scenario). Pairs of volunteers will collaborate<br>remotely on four scenarios, communicating under delayed condition, first via a traditional texting tool<br>(comparable to the one currently used in NASA analog missions) and subsequently via Braiding. During the first<br>two scenarios, volunteers will use the traditional texting tool to get accustomed to delayed communication and<br>have a standard against which to compare Braiding, the tool they will use in the remaining scenarios.<br>Communication between remote team members during the test scenarios will be delayed by up to five minutes<br>one-way. Test scenarios and communication tool will be counterbalanced across pairs of volunteers. A short<br>survey comparing Braiding to the standard texting tool with respect to communication quality, ease of task<br>accomplishment and associated workload will be administered at the end of the HITL evaluation. The<br>communication between remote team members during the scenarios will be examined in terms of length, level of<br>detail and completeness of information provided, and communication efficiency. Individual semi-structured<br>interviews with volunteers will be conducted following the study to assess the value of Braiding and its features.<br><br>RESULTS<br><br>The results of this HITL evaluation are expected to demonstrate the feasibility, acceptability, and benefits of<br>Braiding to facilitate crew/MCC communication under time delay. Improving communication between members<br>of the crew/MCC Multi-Team system will enhance efficiency and joint task success during future long duration<br>space missions.<br><br>
        </div>

